        <main id="main">

            <section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Experimental Research · Thermal Analysis · Python</p><h1>Thermal Diffusion in Digital Composites</h1><p class="subtitle">Graduate Research in the Ni Lab</p><p class="hero-intro">Investigating transient heat transfer in architected liquid-metal composite structures, with current work focused on characterizing their electrical-to-thermal response and surface-temperature evolution.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Duke University</span><span>Jan. 2026 – Present</span><span>Graduate researcher</span></div><p class="status">Ongoing research · Sample fabricated; thermal testing pending</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#current-study">Current study ↓</a><a class="button" href="#earlier-work">Earlier motion-analysis work ↓</a></div></div><aside class="focus-panel" aria-label="Current research focus"><p class="eyebrow">Current Focus</p><h2>Surface Temperature Over Time</h2><p>How does the architecture of a digital-composite element influence the surface response during electrical heating?</p></aside></section>
            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#current-study">Current study</a><a href="#contributions">My work</a><a href="#earlier-work">Earlier research</a></nav>
            <section class="section split" id="current-study"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>Characterize the Thermal Response</h2></div><div class="copy"><p class="lead">Connect electrical heating inside a composite element to the temperature observed at its surface.</p><p>My current research examines thermal diffusion and transient heat transfer in architected digital composites. The study focuses on how material properties and spatial architecture influence heat propagation and surface-temperature evolution.</p><p>I designed mold geometry and fabricated a 4 × 4 mm sample using the lab’s existing fabrication process. This work established practical experience with sample preparation ahead of thermal characterization using the lab’s measurement pipeline.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Study status</strong><p>The sample has been fabricated but has not yet been thermally tested because the thermocouple was unavailable. Measurements of its heating rate and temperature response remain pending. Existing experimental and finite-element results used to contextualize this work were provided by Yang and the Ni Lab; they are separate from measurements of my sample.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Research Work</h2></div><p>My work combines experimental development with Python-based analysis.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Designed mold geometry and fabricated a 4 × 4 mm liquid-metal digital-composite sample using the lab’s established process.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Preparing to characterize the sample using the lab’s existing measurement pipeline, with thermal testing and comparisons across sample sizes still pending.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Previously developed Python video-analysis workflows for soft robotic fish-tail research, including motion tracking and flow characterization.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section" id="earlier-work"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Earlier Motion and Flow Analysis</h2></div><p>Before shifting to the thermal study, I supported the lab's soft robotic fish-tail research through experimental and computer-vision analysis.</p></div><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Stereo tracking</h3><p>Developed camera-calibration and tracking workflows to analyze motion from synchronized video recordings.</p></article><article><h3>Flow characterization</h3><p>Worked with Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV) methods to support analysis of motion and surrounding flow.</p></article><article><h3>Python analysis</h3><p>Processed experimental video and time-series data to examine time-dependent and frequency-domain behavior.</p></article></div><p class="credits">Ni Lab, Duke University. Research adviser: Dr. Ni.</p></section>

